 In a previous article, mwt@iris.bcm.tmc.edu (Man-Wei Tam) writes:
>
>	Could somebody tell me what determines a successful sacrifice?
>	Sometimes I sacrifice the same creature twice in a row and one
>	is consumed but the other is ignored.

Spoilers....


The corpse _has_ to be fresh.  Gods don't like things that are old.  The
fresher the kill is, the better you will do.  If you just killed it and
dragged it over to the alter, you should do all right.  If you've been
carrying it from another room to the alter, it may go stale on you and do
nothing for an alter.  You can also kill things and have their corpses be
too stale to be accepted, even if you just killed it.  It happens, sometimes.
I'm not sure, but I think undead things like orcs and zombies have corpses
that are already old and have a greater tolerance for rotting too fast.
If you _just_ killed it and the alter won't take it, eat it if you are
hungry (assuming you want to put that kind of meat in your mouth).  You
can successfully eat stuff that your god rejects.  Then again, you can always
die from it too. :-)    It all depend on how old the corpse is.  If you want
to guarantee the maximum freshness for any possible corpse, kill it while it is
standing on the alter and then immediately step on the alter and #offer it.
You can do this with big monsters that you can't possibly lift because you are
carrying too much, such as dragons.  Luring them onto the alter is a bit 
tricky, but can be done if you practice.  Sometimes, you may want to dig holes
in a wall to back into if the alter is right next to a wall.  The monster will
follow you on the attack, and be standing right on the alter when you hit it
for the last time.


